---
title: "Design Search Engine"
section: practice
level: advanced
duration: 60 min
description: "Design a web search engine with distributed crawling, indexing, ranking, query serving, freshness, evaluation, and abuse controls."
registryId: practice-search-engine
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is a web search engine?

**A web search engine discovers public documents, builds a searchable representation of
them, and returns a ranked result list for a user's query.** Discovery and indexing happen
asynchronously. Query serving reads a published index snapshot under a strict latency
deadline.

The core invariant is stronger than "return relevant links": **every served result must
come from a valid index snapshot and pass current eligibility policy**. A stale result may
be tolerable during a failure. A removed, malicious, or access-restricted result is not.

This walkthrough uses an illustrative target of 10 billion canonical text documents and
4 billion queries per day. Those are design assumptions for practicing arithmetic, not
claims about any existing search provider.
{% /section-card %}

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Discover and revisit" eyebrow="Crawl" icon="globe" tone="cyan" %}
Maintain a prioritized URL frontier, respect host capacity and crawl policy, fetch
documents, and decide when each URL deserves another visit.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Build searchable evidence" eyebrow="Index" icon="database" tone="violet" %}
Parse and canonicalize content, detect duplicates, build compressed posting lists, and
publish immutable index segments through a versioned manifest.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Retrieve, rank, and filter" eyebrow="Serve" icon="search" tone="green" %}
Retrieve a broad candidate set, score it with lexical and quality signals, enforce hard
policy, generate snippets, and return within the request deadline.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% callout variant="info" %}
New to indexes or capacity math? Review [database indexing](/fundamentals/database-indexing),
[caching](/fundamentals/caching), and [back-of-the-envelope
estimation](/fundamentals/back-of-envelope-calculations) before the deeper sections.
{% /callout %}

{% accordion defaultOpen="clarifying" %}

{% accordion-item id="clarifying" title="1. Clarify requirements and scope" %}

### Turn "search the web" into a bounded contract

Requirements are the user-visible actions and guarantees that the architecture must
support. Clarify them before choosing shards, queues, models, or vendors because one
change in freshness, corpus scope, or policy can reshape the whole system.

{% concept-grid columns="2" %}
{% concept-card title="Core user actions" eyebrow="Functional" icon="search" tone="blue" %}
- Submit keyword, phrase, and filtered text queries.
- Receive ten ranked links with titles, URLs, and generated snippets.
- Correct obvious spelling errors and offer query suggestions.
- Discover HTML and PDF documents from seeds, links, and submitted sitemaps.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Service promises" eyebrow="Non-functional" icon="gauge" tone="green" %}
- Query-serving p95 below 200 ms in supported regions.
- Query-serving availability of 99.99%; indexing availability of 99.9%.
- Publish high-priority updates within five minutes and most accepted changes within 24 hours.
- Keep partial-result and policy-violation rates explicitly measurable.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Correctness and safety" eyebrow="Invariant" icon="shield" tone="amber" %}
- Never serve a document that current policy marks ineligible.
- Do not mix postings, metadata, and ranking features from incompatible index versions.
- Make deletions and legal or security removals override cached result pages.
- Treat fetched documents, links, and structured metadata as untrusted input.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="First-version boundary" eyebrow="Out of scope" icon="boxes" tone="neutral" %}
- No image, video, shopping, advertisement, or generated-answer vertical.
- No per-user behavioral personalization.
- No guarantee that every discovered URL is crawled or indexed.
- No requirement to synchronously rebuild the full index after each document change.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Questions that change the design

1. **Corpus:** Is this a public-web engine, a fixed enterprise collection, or a vertical
   search product? Which languages and document formats are in scope?
2. **Freshness:** Which sources need minute-level updates, and which can be revisited over
   days? Is freshness measured from publication, discovery, fetch, or index visibility?
3. **Quality:** Which query intents matter most, and who provides graded relevance
   judgments for offline evaluation?
4. **Policy:** Which crawler rules, removal requests, malware decisions, and regional
   restrictions can exclude a document?
5. **Degradation:** May the service return partial shard coverage, an older complete
   snapshot, cached head-query results, or an explicit error?
6. **Economics:** What are the storage, network, and ranking-compute budgets per query and
   per accepted document?

{% callout variant="warn" %}
`robots.txt` is a crawler coordination protocol, not authorization. [RFC
9309](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9309.html) defines how compliant crawlers interpret
the Robots Exclusion Protocol; private content still needs real access control.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="capacity" title="2. Estimate scale and constraints" %}

### Convert assumptions into coupled workloads

Capacity estimation translates product assumptions into rates, bytes, and fan-out. It
does not predict an exact fleet size. Server counts come later from representative load
tests with explicit headroom and failure assumptions.

For the baseline scenario, use decimal units, 86,400 seconds per day, and a 3x
peak-to-average query multiplier.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="46.3K/s" label="Average queries" detail="4B queries / 86,400 seconds" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="139K/s" label="Peak queries" detail="3x the average query rate" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="5.8K/s" label="Average revisits" detail="5% of 10B documents per day" tone="green" /%}
{% metric value="60 TB" label="Primary index" detail="30% of a 200 TB extracted-text corpus" tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Explore the workload envelope

Change one assumption at a time, then combine stressors. Notice that a larger corpus
increases both storage and the number of document-shard groups a long-tail query may
contact.

{% interactive-block id="practice/search-engine-capacity-freshness-lab" /%}

### Reproduce the arithmetic

The executable model uses the same explicit assumptions as the lab. Run it with different
inputs before replacing any constant with a production benchmark.

{% code-block file="/api/content/practice/search-engine/code/capacity-model.py" language="python" title="Crawl, index, and query fan-out model" /%}

The baseline calculations are:

1. **Extracted text:** `10B documents x 20 KB = 200 TB` before replicas, metadata,
   postings overhead, raw fetches, or backups.
2. **Inverted index:** an assumed 30% text-to-index ratio gives `60 TB` primary and
   `180 TB` across three serving copies.
3. **Revisit work:** `10B x 5% = 500M` fetches per day, or about `5.8K/s` on average.
4. **Fetch bandwidth:** at an assumed 100 KB response, those revisits transfer about
   `50 TB/day` before retries and rendering.
5. **Query fan-out:** 40 document-shard groups and a 25% whole-result cache hit rate
   produce about `139K x 75% x 40 = 4.2M` shard RPCs per second at peak.

{% callout variant="tip" %}
Popular-query caching lowers head traffic, but it does little for the long tail. Bound
long-tail work with shard pruning, local top-K retrieval, query complexity limits, and
deadline propagation.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="3. Draw the high-level architecture" %}

### Separate asynchronous ingestion from synchronous serving

High-level architecture identifies the critical paths and ownership boundaries before
implementation detail. A crawler or segment merge may take minutes; a user query cannot
wait for either. Connect the two paths only through published, versioned index artifacts.

{% callout variant="info" %}
Sketch the design before reading the reference flows. The grading rubric values a short
query path, a polite and replayable crawler, atomic index publication, bounded fan-out,
and an explicit degraded mode.
{% /callout %}

{% design-challenge
   challengeId="search-engine"
   title="Design the crawl-to-query architecture"
   prompt="Design a text web search engine for 10 billion canonical documents and 139K peak query QPS. Show URL scheduling, polite fetching, parsing and deduplication, immutable index publication, document-shard retrieval, staged ranking, policy enforcement, caching, and behavior when one serving shard or the learned ranker times out." /%}

### Crawl and index path

{% system-flow title="From discovered URL to published index" caption="Every stage is replayable. A document becomes searchable only after its segment and metadata version appear in an atomic manifest." %}
{% system-node title="URL frontier" eyebrow="Schedule" icon="route" tone="blue" %}
Prioritize by host, importance, change probability, and next eligible fetch time while
preserving per-host politeness state.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Fetcher fleet" eyebrow="Retrieve" icon="globe" tone="cyan" %}
Resolve policy, acquire a host lease, fetch within byte and time limits, and record the
HTTP outcome without following unbounded redirects.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Parse and canonicalize" eyebrow="Normalize" icon="code" tone="amber" %}
Extract text, links, language, metadata, and canonical hints; reject malformed or
dangerous content and compute exact and near-duplicate fingerprints.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Document and link stores" eyebrow="Persist" icon="database" tone="violet" %}
Keep fetched blobs, parsed document versions, graph edges, policy state, and processing
lineage independently of the serving index.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Segment builder" eyebrow="Index" icon="layers" tone="rose" %}
Analyze fields, write compressed postings and forward metadata, compute batch features,
and verify checksums and document counts.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Manifest publisher" eyebrow="Release" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Atomically publish compatible segments, deletion generations, and feature versions, then
warm serving replicas before shifting queries.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Query-serving path

{% system-flow title="From query to ranked results" caption="Each hop receives the remaining deadline. Logging and model training consume events asynchronously and cannot block the response." %}
{% system-node title="Edge and admission" eyebrow="Protect" icon="shield" tone="blue" %}
Authenticate paid APIs where needed, rate-limit abusive clients, normalize locale, and
reject requests that exceed a query-complexity budget.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Query understanding" eyebrow="Interpret" icon="search" tone="cyan" %}
Tokenize, detect language and intent, apply bounded spelling or synonym expansion, and
check a version-aware whole-result cache.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Shard broker" eyebrow="Retrieve" icon="network" tone="violet" %}
Route to one healthy replica per relevant document-shard group, apply per-shard
deadlines, and request only a bounded local top-K.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Candidate merge" eyebrow="Aggregate" icon="workflow" tone="amber" %}
Merge local candidates, deduplicate canonical documents, record shard coverage, and
prepare features for a much smaller global set.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Staged ranking" eyebrow="Score" icon="activity" tone="rose" %}
Apply lexical retrieval, inexpensive quality signals, and a richer learned ranker only
to the final hundreds of candidates.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Policy and response" eyebrow="Release" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Recheck current eligibility, generate safe snippets, attach freshness and coverage
metadata, cache eligible output, and return.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

The original [Google search architecture
paper](https://research.google/pubs/the-anatomy-of-a-large-scale-hypertextual-web-search-engine/)
is useful historical research on crawling, indexing, link structure, and query serving.
Treat it as a foundation for reasoning, not a description of any current production
system.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="data" title="4. Model data and choose storage" %}

### Design records around access patterns and lifecycle

A data model defines identity, versioning, and ownership before it defines a database
vendor. Search needs several stores because URL scheduling, immutable posting scans,
document lookup, graph processing, and policy deletion have different access patterns.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="URL frontier" icon="route" tone="blue" %}
Key work by normalized host and `next_fetch_at`. Store priority, retry state, robots
version, lease owner, and last outcome. Leasing prevents duplicate concurrent fetches.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Document store" icon="braces" tone="cyan" %}
Map a stable `doc_id` to canonical URL, content hash, fetch version, parsed fields,
language, timestamps, and current eligibility state.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Blob store" icon="storage" tone="neutral" %}
Keep bounded raw responses and large parsed artifacts in object storage for replay,
auditing, and parser upgrades. Tier or delete them by explicit retention policy.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Inverted index" icon="database" tone="violet" %}
Map each analyzed term to compressed postings containing document IDs, term frequency,
positions, and field information for one document-shard segment.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Link and feature stores" icon="network" tone="amber" %}
Represent canonical document edges separately and publish computed authority, quality,
language, and spam features under a version compatible with the index snapshot.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Policy ledger" icon="shield" tone="rose" %}
Record removals, malware decisions, expiry, provenance, appeal state, and deletion
generation. This ledger can invalidate caches faster than a full index rebuild.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Choose document sharding for the baseline

Partition canonical documents across shard groups by a stable hash or managed range. Each
group owns the complete term dictionary and postings for its document subset. A query can
run independently on every selected group, return local top-K candidates, and merge them.

Term sharding sends each query term to a different owner and then performs distributed
posting intersections. It can reduce duplicated term dictionaries, but multi-term and
phrase queries require more cross-shard coordination. It is not the baseline here.

### Publish snapshots, not partial mutations

- Builders write immutable segments, checksums, document counts, analyzer versions, and
  feature versions to durable storage.
- Validators open the complete candidate snapshot and run retrieval, policy, and
  compatibility checks before publication.
- A small strongly consistent manifest changes from generation `N` to `N+1` atomically.
- Query replicas pin one manifest generation for the full request, even during rollout.
- Tombstones and urgent policy deny-lists suppress removed documents until compaction
  physically drops their postings.
- Old generations remain available long enough for rollback and in-flight requests, then
  are reclaimed by reference count and retention policy.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Never treat the result cache as the source of truth for eligibility. Cache entries need a
snapshot version and a policy version, and urgent removals need direct invalidation or a
final query-time deny check.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="deep-dive" title="5. Deep-dive the critical components" %}

### Inspect the mechanisms that dominate quality and risk

A critical component owns a constraint the rest of the design cannot repair later. For
search, those constraints are polite discovery, consistent index publication, bounded
retrieval and ranking, and resistance to adversarial content and traffic.

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Crawling" %}
### Schedule by host, value, and change probability

The frontier is not one FIFO queue. Partition by normalized host so one worker coordinates
connection limits, robots policy, retries, and adaptive backoff for that host. Within the
allowed work, prioritize URLs by expected search value, estimated change rate, source
quality, depth, and time since last successful fetch.

- Fetch and cache `robots.txt` according to RFC 9309 before scheduling allowed paths.
- Use bounded response bytes, redirect hops, rendering time, and links per page to contain
  crawler traps and decompression bombs.
- Normalize URLs, honor defensible canonical hints, and remove tracking parameters before
  adding new work.
- Detect exact duplicates with content hashes and near duplicates with shingles plus an
  approximate fingerprint; keep one canonical document and merge useful signals.
- Learn recrawl intervals from observed change history. Breaking sources may receive
  minutes; stable reference pages may receive weeks.
- Back off on timeouts, throttling, and server errors. More crawler capacity does not grant
  permission to overload a publisher.

Google's current [crawl-budget documentation](https://developers.google.com/crawling/docs/crawl-budget)
describes capacity and demand as separate constraints. The same distinction is useful in
this vendor-neutral design.
{% /tab %}

{% tab title="Indexing and freshness" %}
### Make new content visible without rewriting the world

Analyzers tokenize text, normalize case and language-specific forms, and emit fielded
terms. Posting lists store sorted document IDs plus term frequency and optional positions;
delta encoding and block compression reduce bytes and improve sequential scans.

Use two index lanes:

1. **Delta lane:** build small segments continuously for high-priority accepted changes.
   Publish them frequently so news and urgent updates become searchable within minutes.
2. **Base lane:** compact many immutable segments in the background, recompute expensive
   global features, and periodically publish a cleaner snapshot.

Queries read the base plus a bounded number of delta segments. Excessive tiny segments
increase seeks and duplicate work, so compaction is part of serving health, not only a
storage task. Measure freshness from source publication when available, then separately
track discovery lag, fetch lag, processing lag, and publish lag.
{% /tab %}

{% tab title="Retrieval and ranking" %}
### Spend compute in stages

The inverted index retrieves candidates with a lexical function such as BM25, which
rewards term match while controlling repeated-term saturation and document-length bias.
[Lucene's BM25 documentation](https://lucene.apache.org/core/10_2_2/core/org/apache/lucene/search/similarities/package-summary.html)
is an official implementation reference.

1. Each document shard uses lexical matching, phrase positions, and cheap static quality
   signals to return a local top-K.
2. The broker merges and deduplicates local candidates, then applies global authority,
   freshness, locale, and lightweight intent features.
3. A learned ranker scores only the final hundreds of candidates under a fixed time and
   feature budget.
4. Deterministic policy removes ineligible results, applies domain or duplicate limits,
   and chooses snippets. Policy is not another soft ranking feature.

Propagate one request deadline. If an optional feature misses its budget, use a versioned
default or an earlier-stage score. Never let an unbounded feature fan-out consume the
whole query latency target.
{% /tab %}

{% tab title="Abuse and integrity" %}
### Assume publishers and clients can optimize against the system

- **Index spam:** detect keyword stuffing, doorway pages, link farms, cloaking, copied
  content, malicious downloads, and sudden coordinated graph changes.
- **Crawler attacks:** sandbox parsers, cap decompression and rendering, validate network
  destinations, and prevent fetched pages from reaching internal or link-local services.
- **Query abuse:** rate-limit by risk and account, cap terms and expansions, bound regex or
  filter complexity, and shed anonymous expensive queries before healthy traffic.
- **Signal manipulation:** exclude known bots from click labels, detect coordinated click
  patterns, preserve randomized exploration data, and audit training lineage.
- **Policy mistakes:** require reason codes, reviewer provenance, appeal workflows,
  versioned rules, false-positive measurement, and independent emergency rollback.

Abuse controls have two error classes: missed harmful content and legitimate content
incorrectly removed. Monitor both by language, region, source cohort, and rule version.
{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}

### Practice the ranking boundary

Change query intent and signal weights, then disable the hard gate. The important lesson
is not one universal weight vector; it is the separation between graded relevance and
non-negotiable eligibility.

{% interactive-block
   id="practice/search-engine-ranking-policy-lab"
   dataFile="/api/content/practice/search-engine/data/ranking-scenarios.json" /%}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="reliability" title="6. Plan scalability, reliability, and evaluation" %}

### Protect the query path while slower pipelines recover

Reliability is the ability to keep user-visible promises through partial failure. Scale
the crawler, builders, query brokers, retrieval shards, ranking service, and caches
independently so pressure in one path does not cascade into the others.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="99.99%" label="Query availability" detail="Measured by region and query class" tone="green" /%}
{% metric value="<200 ms" label="Query p95" detail="Includes retrieval, ranking, policy, and network" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="<5 min" label="Priority freshness" detail="Source publication to searchable result" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="0" label="Policy violations" detail="A correctness target, not an error-budget metric" tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Define failure behavior before incidents

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Serving replica loss" icon="server" tone="blue" %}
Route the shard group to a healthy replica, hedge only slow tail requests within a budget,
and rebuild redundancy without overloading the remaining copies.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Shard timeout" icon="timer" tone="amber" %}
Stop at the request deadline. Return partial candidates only when coverage remains above a
defined threshold; record missing groups and avoid caching low-coverage output.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Ranker outage" icon="activity" tone="violet" %}
Fall back to the last compatible lightweight ranker or lexical score. Continue current
policy checks and expose the fallback rate to operators.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Bad index generation" icon="database" tone="rose" %}
Stop rollout on checksum, document-count, latency, quality, or policy regressions. Pin
replicas to the previous complete manifest and investigate offline.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Crawler backlog" icon="route" tone="cyan" %}
Preserve host leases and durable frontier state, lower low-value recrawl priority, and keep
urgent deletion or security work on a separate bounded lane.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Regional outage" icon="globe" tone="green" %}
Route queries to another region with an older complete snapshot if necessary. State the
freshness and latency cost; never bypass policy to claim availability.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Evaluate retrieval and the complete product

Search quality needs labeled information needs, candidate documents, and graded relevance
judgments. [NIST's TREC program](https://trec.nist.gov/howto.html) documents test
collections, topics, runs, pooling, and relevance judgments used in information-retrieval
evaluation.

- **Retrieval:** Recall@K confirms that relevant documents enter the candidate set.
- **Ranking:** NDCG@10 rewards placing highly relevant documents near the top; MRR helps
  navigational queries where the first correct result matters most.
- **Freshness:** measure publication-to-index lag and stale-result rate by source priority.
- **Policy and abuse:** measure violation rate, false positives, appeal reversals, spam in
  top results, and time to urgent removal.
- **System health:** measure p50, p95, and p99 latency by stage; shard coverage; timeout and
  fallback rates; cache age; segment count; and index-generation skew.
- **User outcomes:** monitor successful-result interactions, reformulation, abandonment,
  and explicit satisfaction by intent. Treat clicks as biased observations, not labels by
  themselves.

### Release changes with layered evidence

1. Evaluate on frozen, time-separated query sets with graded judgments and critical slices.
2. Replay production-like query mixes against a complete candidate index generation.
3. Shadow the new retrieval, ranker, or policy version without changing user results.
4. Canary by region and query class with automatic rollback on latency, coverage, quality,
   or policy gates.
5. Run an online experiment only after defining the primary metric, guardrails, sample
   unit, and stopping rule.

Alert on error-budget burn and sustained quality movement, not every isolated noisy query.
Keep model, feature, analyzer, policy, and index versions in every trace so operators can
attribute a regression.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="tradeoffs" title="7. Compare trade-offs and alternatives" %}

### Make every optimization name its cost

A trade-off exchanges one desirable property for another under a stated workload. Avoid
calling a choice "best" without naming the query mix, freshness target, failure model, and
measurement that justify it.

{% concept-grid columns="2" %}
{% concept-card title="Document vs term sharding" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Document sharding makes each shard independently retrieve local top-K but fans long-tail
queries across groups. Term sharding narrows term ownership but makes multi-term and phrase
intersection a distributed operation.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Freshness vs segment efficiency" icon="timer" tone="green" %}
Frequent delta publication lowers visibility lag but creates more segments, cache churn,
and merge pressure. Batch rebuilds are compact but make urgent changes wait.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Completeness vs tail latency" icon="gauge" tone="amber" %}
Waiting for every shard improves coverage but lets one slow group control p99. A deadline
protects latency but requires measured partial-result quality and a minimum coverage gate.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Precomputation vs adaptability" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
Static authority and quality features are cheap at query time but update slowly. Dynamic
features adapt to intent and events but add latency, cost, and failure dependencies.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Lexical vs hybrid retrieval" icon="search" tone="cyan" %}
Lexical retrieval is efficient, precise for rare terms, and explainable. Vector candidates
can improve semantic recall but add index memory, approximate-search tuning, and new abuse
and evaluation surfaces.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Caching vs freshness" icon="cache" tone="rose" %}
Whole-result caching removes repeated head work but can preserve stale ranking or policy.
Short TTLs, version keys, removal invalidation, and final eligibility checks constrain risk.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Alternatives rejected for the baseline

- **One mutable global index:** simple to describe, but partial writes expose inconsistent
  versions and recovery is difficult. Immutable segments plus manifests isolate rollout.
- **A synchronous crawl-to-query pipeline:** appears fresh, but user latency and
  availability would depend on fetchers, parsers, and builders.
- **A vector-only index:** loses strong exact-term behavior and makes web-scale memory,
  filtering, and explainability harder. Add semantic retrieval as a measured candidate
  source, not a replacement by default.
- **One large learned ranker for every document:** spends expensive inference before
  retrieval has reduced the corpus. Staged ranking gives richer models a bounded input.
- **Clicks as the only relevance signal:** confounds position, presentation, bot traffic,
  and the previous ranker's exposure policy. Preserve human judgments and controlled
  experiments.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="wrap-up" title="8. Wrap up, risks, and extensions" %}

### Restate the design as four owned loops

A wrap-up proves that the components form one coherent system. Trace discovery, index
publication, query serving, and measurement without adding a new dependency in the final
minute.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Schedule bounded discovery" label="Crawl" %}
Prioritize a durable host-partitioned frontier, respect crawl policy and server health,
contain traps, and persist replayable fetch outcomes.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Publish complete snapshots" label="Index" %}
Canonicalize and deduplicate documents, build immutable base and delta segments, validate
versions, and atomically advance the serving manifest.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Retrieve and rank in stages" label="Serve" %}
Interpret the query, fan out with deadlines, merge local top-K candidates, apply bounded
ranking, enforce policy, and generate the response.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Measure and improve safely" label="Operate" %}
Join system traces, relevance judgments, freshness, abuse reviews, and experiments to
release changes gradually and roll back each layer independently.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Highest remaining risks

1. A crawler trap or parser exploit consumes the fetch fleet or reaches an internal
   network destination.
2. A bad manifest combines incompatible postings, document metadata, features, or policy
   generations.
3. Query fan-out or hedging multiplies load during a shard or regional incident.
4. Spam and coordinated behavior corrupt both the index and the learning signals used to
   rank it.
5. A whole-result cache serves content after an urgent removal or across incompatible
   policy versions.
6. Offline relevance gains do not transfer to tail queries, languages, regions, or live
   user outcomes.

### Extensions worth rehearsing

- **Real-time news lane**
  - Add trusted push discovery, smaller delta segments, and minute-level publication.
  - Measure duplicate-event bursts, source authority, correction handling, and merge debt.
- **Hybrid semantic retrieval**
  - Build a separate vector candidate source with metadata filtering and a fixed latency
    budget.
  - Evaluate incremental Recall@K and NDCG gains by intent before paying the global cost.
- **Multilingual search**
  - Add language-specific analyzers, cross-language retrieval, locale-aware judgments, and
    independent policy coverage.
  - Keep analyzer and embedding versions explicit in every segment manifest.
- **Image and video verticals**
  - Introduce media extraction, perceptual deduplication, modality-specific indexes, and
    separate ranking and safety evaluation.
  - Merge vertical results through a calibrated policy instead of one incomparable score.
- **Private enterprise search**
  - Replace public eligibility with document-level authorization evaluated at query time.
  - Propagate source permissions and deletions faster than content indexing while avoiding
    permission-based existence leaks.

### Primary references

- [RFC 9309: Robots Exclusion Protocol](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9309.html) for
  crawler access rules, error handling, caching, and security considerations.
- [Google Crawling Infrastructure: crawl budget](https://developers.google.com/crawling/docs/crawl-budget)
  for an official description of crawl capacity and crawl demand.
- [The Anatomy of a Large-Scale Hypertextual Web Search
  Engine](https://research.google/pubs/the-anatomy-of-a-large-scale-hypertextual-web-search-engine/)
  for the foundational research architecture and link-based ranking context.
- [Apache Lucene similarity documentation](https://lucene.apache.org/core/10_2_2/core/org/apache/lucene/search/similarities/package-summary.html)
  for an official BM25 implementation reference.
- [NIST TREC](https://trec.nist.gov/howto.html) for test collections, pooled relevance
  judgments, retrieval runs, and evaluation practice.

{% callout variant="tip" %}
End an interview by restating the index-publication invariant, tracing one crawl and one
query, naming the most dangerous overload path, defending one trade-off, and identifying
the first metric that would force the design to change.
{% /callout %}

